  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 255 PM EST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nCurrently-Tonight... KMLB radar imagery shows dry weather across\neast central Florida. GOES-19 satellite imagery and local\nobservations indicate mostly to partly sunny skies. Temperatures\nare currently in the low to upper 60s with dew points in the low\n30s to mid 40s. Winds are from the north at 5-15mph with gusts to\n20-25mph. Analysis charts show expansive high pressure to the\nnorth of east central Florida (~1029mb) building over the central\nCONUS to the Midwest. Dry weather is expected tonight. Expect\nanother cold night with low temperatures in the low to upper 40s\nalong and north of I-4 and the upper 40s to mid 50s to the south.\nApparent temperatures will be close to cold weather shelter\nthresholds but are expected to stay just at or above 40F north of\nI-4 with the low to upper 40s inland north and west of the \nTreasure Coast, as well as in the near the coast north of\nTitusville. The mid 40s to mid 50s are forecast elsewhere. There\nis a 0-50% chance for apparent temperatures to reach 40F or\nbelow, 0-30% for portions of Orange, 0-30% for portions of Lake,\nand 0-20% for portions of Volusia county. If the aforementioned\ncriteria is met, then the apparent temperature threshold is \nlikely to only occur for approximately 1-2 hours. \n\nSunday-Monday... The dry weather is expected to persist through\nthe weekend as high pressure builds to the north over the eastern\nCONUS. Rain chances increase over the local Atlantic waters and\nnear the coast into the workweek as onshore flow develops, as well\nas an upper level trough deepens to the northwest of east central\nFlorida over the Deep South. Below normal temperatures are\nexpected with highs in the low to upper 70s, as well as lows in \nthe low to mid 50s north of I-4 and the mid 50s to mid 60s to the\nsouth. \n\nTuesday-Friday... Guidance has come into better agreement with the\nmid to long term forecast. There are still some differences in the\nstrength of a trough/low that is forecast to track northeast over\nthe western Atlantic and how close the trough/low gets to the\neastern Florida coast which would make a difference on the level\nof coastal and marine impacts east central Florida is expected to\nexperience mid to late week, as well as available moisture for\neast central Florida. The LREF-clusters are in fairly good \nagreement on the associated 500mb trough deepening over the state \nof Florida, in addition to the northern Florida and east central \nFlorida Atlantic waters. Regardless, rain chances are expected to\nincrease on Tuesday with scattered to numerous showers and a low \nchance of lightning. Rain chances are expected to remain above \nnormal each day through the end of the workweek. Additionally, \nbreezy to potentially windy conditions are forecast each day, as \nwell as a coastal and marine impacts. Temperatures are forecast to\nbe seasonable for highs and seasonable to below normal for lows.",
